            zero (check:plans run in the checkout, because wk needs the repository), lint:rules at zero, faults 52
            of 52.

    T71
        title       UI primitives, first wave: command palette and number input fixtures
        status      done
        priority    medium
        description
            Build the command palette and number input as fixtures, each with ui-sdk's behaviour scenarios ported as
            Story tests.

            **Why now:** today's fixtures share one domain, a write with an unknown ending. The user chose ui-sdk's most
            stateful primitives as the source of fixture variety and, later, of Flow's official demo (the user,
            2026-11-16). These two run on production today, so they land before @task.V3.T70 and the generator runs
            over them.

            **Read first:** code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/ui-primitives-generality-2026-11-16.md (the
            machines to build, sections on the command palette and number input),
            code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/ui-primitives-2026-11-16.md (behaviours and edge cases), the
            upstream tests in `codebases/ui-sdk/packages/core/test/command.test.ts` (25) and `number-input.test.ts`
            (12), at commit `98ba9fd`.

            **Risk:** the idiom pass (code:wiki/plans/flow/references/proposals/ui-primitives-idiom-2026-11-16.md) found
            the `Configure` props workaround leaky; the user reviews its proposals separately, so build with today's
            features and note each wiring line it costs. ui-sdk is Apache-2.0: behaviour is ported, never code or test
            text, and the package never names or reads `codebases/`.

            **Approach:** one fixture folder each in the four-file shape, with a usual and a stress Story. Every
            upstream core test becomes a Story test asserting the same behaviour in our words, cited by our Spec or
            Story id; a scenario no Story can express (DOM focus, pointer geometry, rendering) is listed as dropped with
            its reason. React-only and SSR tests are out of scope here (@task.V3.T30).

            May change: `fixtures/command-palette/`, `fixtures/number-input/`, `tests/`. Evidence: this Task's comments.

            False pass: a ported test that asserts less than its upstream case → caught by the case-by-case mapping
            comment and by `nub run faults`.

            User's read: each dropped upstream scenario with its reason; each workaround's wiring lines.
        acceptance
            - `fixtures/command-palette/` and `fixtures/number-input/` exist in the four-file shape, each with a usual
              and a stress Story, built with today's public API.
            - Every core test of ui-sdk's command (25) and number input (12) maps to a Story test asserting the same
              behaviour, or is listed as dropped with its reason, in one mapping comment on this Task.
            - The run rules apply to every new Story test, and the reload sweep covers the new Stories.
            - Each new runtime rule a scenario relies on has a planted fault that `nub run faults` catches; a dead core
              fails every new test.
            - The package never imports, reads or names `codebases/`; `nub run check` and `nub run lint:rules` pass.

            - R1 (acceptance 1; Task approach): `fixtures/command-palette/` and `fixtures/number-input/` hold
              `contracts.ts`, `machine.ts`, `server.ts`, `stories.ts`, import only `src/index.ts` and
              `src/host/testing/index.ts`, and export `stories` with `usual` and `stress`; `flow.config.ts` lists both.
            - R2 (acceptance 2; the user: "copy the scenarios from ui-sdk's tests"): each of the 37 upstream cases below
              has a test in `tests/command-palette/` or `tests/number-input/` titled with its Story id, or a drop
              reason; the mapping comment says what each asserts and flags any partial port.
            - R3 (acceptance 3; code:packages/flow-state-v3/tests/rules/played.ts): every new Story test runs through
              `played`; `tests/rules/sweep.test.ts` sweeps both fixtures' `stories`.
            - R4 (acceptance 4): each Turn or WriteLanes rule a scenario needs and `tests/faults.ts` lacks gets a fault
              (expected: Turn R14 armOn restart and exit stop, Turn R2 authored order with Denied reasons); `nub run
              faults` catches each, and the dead core fails every new test.
            - R5 (acceptance 5): `grep -ri 'codebases\|ui-sdk'` over the package finds nothing; check and lint:rules
              at zero.
            - R6 (brief risk): no prop in an actor's identity; props arrive by a `Configure`-style event, and each
              wiring line it costs is logged here.
            - R7 (constraint "test the spec"): expected values come from the upstream case or our Spec; where upstream
              or the proposal sketch looks wrong, the case and the pinned behaviour are logged here.
        comment     2026-11-15T22:05:01Z
            Decisions (agent, small): the palette persists (coordinator's ruling) and the number input does not. A fifth
            file, `commands.ts`, holds the palette's list Schema and ranking. Stress Stories run 150 gestures and the
            sweep puts its reload at every fifth step of a `/stress` Story, since those already reload and a full sweep
            ran past the test timeout. Reverse: drop `strideOf` in `tests/rules/sweep.test.ts`.
        comment     2026-11-15T22:05:02Z
            Pinned against upstream or the sketch (R7): the palette keeps one error per command; upstream keeps only the
            last, so a second command's failure erases the first one's reason, against its own doc ("until the next
            run"). Leaving the field clamps and snaps the typed text, as upstream does ("70" in 1..10 commits 10); the
            sketch reformatted the old value. Commits are `lastCommit { seq, value }`, since a level `committed` cannot
            tell two commits of 6 apart (N10) or a controller step from a commit (N11). A hold steps at 400 ms, as the
            sketch's text says; its machine did not. An unknown command may be run once more (`retryable`, times 1).
        comment     2026-11-15T22:05:03Z
            Wiring cost of today's features (for the user's idiom review). Number input: a `Configure { settings }`
            event, one memory mirror of seven props, one handler; the default value is a `Set`; every Story re-sends
            `Configure` after a reload. Palette: `Configure { commands, autoHighlight, scores }`, three memory mirrors
            persisted with the actor, one handler, re-sent after each reload. A scoring function cannot be event data,
            so it arrives as its table of `scores`. The failure ending names its command through a `CommandFailed` error
            and one `catchTag` in the commit, because built `.from` sees only the ending's value.
        comment     2026-11-15T22:05:04Z
