    T75
        title       Fast test commands: stress behind a flag, faults in parallel
        status      done
        priority    high
        description
            Make the test commands fast: a fast suite by default, the stress runs behind a flag, and `nub run faults` in
            parallel with each fault's target test first.

            **Why now:** `nub run faults` plants 52 faults and the dead core one after another, each over the whole
            suite, and takes 5 to 10 minutes; the stress Stories, their reload sweeps and the generated stress seeds
            slow the package suite too. Both grow with every later Task. The user asked for an optimisation pass and for
            big test commands to run rarely, with stress as a flag (the user, 2026-11-16).

            **Read first:** code:tools/faults/run.ts, code:packages/flow-state-v3/tests/faults.ts,
            `tests/rules/sweep.test.ts`, `tests/generated/`, `vite.config.ts`, the Epic's `instructions` (Speed; Big
            test commands run rarely).

            **Risk:** speed must not weaken a gate. Every fault still ends caught or missed by the same tests, the dead
            core still gets a full run, and a stress run behind the flag is unchanged when the flag is on.

            **Approach:** measure first (time per file, per fault, per command), then: plant up to 8 faults at once,
            each in its own scratch copy, within about 16 GB; run each fault's named target tests first and fall back to
            the full suite only when they pass; put the stress runs behind one flag and one command (`nub run
            test:stress`); keep the fast suite free of anything slower than its budget.

            May change: `tools/faults/`, `tests/`, `vite.config.ts`, `package.json` scripts. Evidence: this
            Task's comments.

            False pass: a faster faults run that stops catching a fault → caught by the same caught list, fault by
            fault, before and after, and by the dead core's full run.
        acceptance
            - Timings before and after are recorded in a Task comment: the fast suite, `nub run test:stress`, and `nub
              run faults`.
            - The default package suite excludes the stress runs; `nub run test:stress` runs them unchanged, and a test
              shows the flag selects exactly the stress files.
            - `nub run faults` plants faults in parallel, tries each fault's target tests first, and gives the same
              caught list fault by fault as before; the dead core still runs the full suite, and a missed fault still
              exits non-zero.
            - `nub run faults` finishes in under 3 minutes at today's fault count, and the fast suite in under 60
              seconds, on this machine.
            - `nub run check` and `nub run lint:rules` pass.
        comment     2026-11-16T01:52:22Z
            Timings at a129f164, alone (before at e82d2c0a): fast suite 18.5 s to 10.5 s; test:stress 5.2 s (none
            before; stress ran in the fast suite); faults 206 s to 25 s. Caught list identical fault by fault: 52 of 52
            before and after, every fault now caught by its targets, none by the whole suite; dead core runs the whole
            suite, 178 green before and after, all explained.
        comment     2026-11-16T02:03:57Z
            PASS at 96ef4847 (independent check-task). Gates on a git archive copy: check and lint:rules at zero; fast
            suite 452 plus 8 it.fails in 10.2 s; test:stress 8 files, 23 tests, 4.5 s, selection test green; faults exit
            0 in 25 s (52 of 52, dead core whole suite, all green explained). Caught list: the 52 faults' edits are the
            same multiset before and after, the 6 moved ones are spread into faults; a missed, stale or unjudged fault,
            a missing target file or a red unchanged run still exits 1; scratch copies are removed (none left).
            Target-first probe: a target load error is NOT JUDGED and falls back to the whole suite; a setup throw
            inside a test counts caught, as under the old --bail=1 whole-suite run, and the whole-suite control guards
            it, so no weakening. F2 to F4 fixed with checks (planted 60 s timeout; ~reloaded checkpoint asserted so
            Restore I1 cuts; before() throws, tested). Not blocking: F5 half open, Stories T5 still names reason
            Advance, which R6 and run.ts do not have; tools/stress/select.test.ts's 'default file runs stress material'
            guard misses stress Stories run once in tests/cli/cli.test.ts and tests/stories/story.test.ts and the
            stress-profile checks in tests/generated/profiles.test.ts and planted.test.ts (cheap, inside budget); nub
            run verify no longer runs any stress file. Sidecars: gate-weakening (accepted: target-alone false catch is
            theoretical, changed to non-blocking; faults list parity accepted) and blind (stress-in-default and verify
            findings accepted as non-blocking; 'stress run not unchanged' rejected: the only change is F3's
